{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>While Israel dwelled in that land.</b> All these befell him before he came to Hebron, to Yitzchak." "<b>And he lay.</b> Because he disarranged the bed, Scripture charges him as though he had lain with her. And why did he disarrange and profane his couch? When Rachel died, Yaakov took his bed, which was always placed in Rachel's tent and not in the other tents, and set it in Bilhah's tent. Reuven came and demanded his mother's humiliation, saying: If my mother's sister was a rival to my mother, shall my mother's sister's handmaid be a rival to my mother? Therefore he disarranged it (Shabbat 55)." "<b>And the sons of Yaakov were twelve.</b> It returns to the first matter: since Binyamin was born, the bed was complete, and from now on they were fit to be counted — and it counts them. And our Sages interpreted it to teach that they were all equal and all righteous — that Reuven did not sin."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:02:11.977076794Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}